"""Facts for the 53 equipment reference sheets (docs/research/equipment): a representative real device per item, its dimensions with the source each came from, and what the model must show.

Source levels (honest about what was actually seen on 10 October 2026):
  datasheet = the manufacturer's data sheet or brochure was opened and the figure read in it;
  listing   = the figure is the manufacturer's specification as printed on a distributor, catalogue or encyclopaedic page returned by a search (the page text was read; the manufacturer PDF was not opened);
  standard  = a published standard fixes the size (for example ISO 216 paper sizes);
  open      = no source was found: the number is not given and the model uses a placeholder until one is.
A dimension marked 'approx' is a typical value from the cited page, not a single manufacturer's figure. Nothing here is guessed: unfound values stay 'open'.
"""
from __future__ import annotations


def d(part, value, source, url, level, exact="exact"):
    return {"part": part, "value": value, "source": source, "url": url, "level": level, "exact": exact}


